<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad bin Thani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1788–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-thani">House of Thani</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad bin Thani was the leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doha">Doha</a> and the first ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a> recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. After a destructive war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> and Abu Dhabi, he signed an agreement with the British envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewis-pelly">Lewis Pelly</a> in 1868 that treated him as the peninsula's leader and separated Qatar from Bahraini claims. His son Jassim, who succeeded him, is regarded as the founder of the Qatari state.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born in Fuwayrit, the second eldest son of Sheikh Thani bin Mohammad. He began to gain influence over the peninsula after his family moved from Fuwayrit to Al Bidda in 1848. Until then each tribe and settlement had its own leader, and with his arrival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a> began to gain economic and political weight, though it remained under the suzerainty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In May 1851 Faisal bin Turki, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>, marched on Al Bidda as a base for invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>. Muhammad and his son were among the leaders of the Qatari forces in the Battle of Mesaimeer from 2 to 4 June 1851, during which the Bahraini and Abu Dhabi forces withdrew to their ships. He then made a separate peace with Faisal, accepting Wahhabi rule on condition that he remained chief of Al Bidda, and on 8 June his men took the Burj Al-Maah watchtower, whereupon the allied commanders fled to Bahrain. Under a treaty of 25 July 1851 negotiated by Saeed bin Tahnun, Faisal renounced his claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a> and Muhammad gave up his position.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The enmity left by the battle fed into the Qatari–Bahraini War of 1867, and on 12 September 1868 a treaty signed with the British representative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewis-pelly">Lewis Pelly</a> marked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatar">Qatar</a>'s emergence as a separate political entity. In 1871 he asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at Al Hasa for protection against outside attack, though in the same decade the Ottomans themselves turned hostile to the Qataris. He died of natural causes on 18 December 1878, a date now kept as a national day in Qatar. He had six sons and two daughters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mohammed bin Thani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a destructive war between them, the British envoy Lewis Pelly signed an agreement with <b>Muhammad bin Thani of Doha</b> in 1868, recognising him as the peninsula's leader.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-qatar">Sheikhdom of Qatar</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fuwayrit, in what is now <a href="/countries/qatar">Qatar</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doha">Doha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/qatar">Qatar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.02,51.37&amp;year=1846" aria-label="Fuwayrit on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muhammad bin Thani"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4339 5315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4339 5315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.02,51.37&amp;year=1846">What was here in 1846? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
